{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And that prophet, or dreamer of dreams, etc.</b> Scripture needed to say both נָבִיא and חוֹלֵם, for if it had said only נָבִיא, I would say that Scripture did not impose the death penalty except upon one who said that HaShem spoke to him in prophecy — since Israel is commanded to heed a prophet, as it is written (Deuteronomy 18:15): ״אֵלָיו תִּשְׁמָעוּן״ — which is not so regarding one who comes with words of dreams, for there is no obligation upon Israel to heed words of dreams, and there is room to say of them ״חֲלוֹמוֹת שָׁוְא יְדַבֵּרוּ״ — he is not [automatically] liable; therefore Scripture says ״אוֹ חוֹלֵם״. And if it had said ״חוֹלֵם״ etc. and not נָבִיא, I would say that only the dreamer is liable to death, but not the prophet — and all the more a prophet who is established as a true prophet, as I explained in the preceding verse, whose words would be believed just as his words are believed when he tells them to transgress other commandments for the time being — therefore Scripture says ״וְהַנָּבִיא״ etc." "<b>That dreamer shall be put to death, for he spoke, etc.</b> The intention of Scripture is as follows, according to what one would say: Granted the prophet — his prophecy surely did not come in such a manner, so he fabricated it from his own heart; but this is not so with the dreamer, since in a dream wondrous and false things may come to a person. It is possible that this man actually dreamed this dream and did not lie. And it is true that if the death penalty applied for his telling them, עִבְדוּ עֲבוֹדָה זָרָה, this one would be liable under the law of a seducer (מֵסִית); but since he is executed for the telling of the dream alone — and as I explained, even if the matters are definitive, he says nothing except that the sign is that one should worship [idols] — if so, why should the dreamer die for his dream? Therefore it says <b>כִּי דִבֶּר סָרָה</b> — meaning, even though one could justly say that the dream did come according to his words (and this is what He alluded to in the word ״הַחֲלוֹם,״ meaning, even supposing it were true that his dream was as he said), even so, he should not have regarded it and recounted it to the people of Israel, since he spoke rebellion (סָרָה) against HaShem — and for this alone he shall be put to death." "<b>And He concludes</b> saying <b>who brought you out of the land of Egypt</b> — Scripture comes to judge with righteousness the liability of the dreamer's death penalty through this statement, in the following manner: Since HaShem your Elohim brought you out of the land of Egypt, HaShem has shown with an open eye, in waking, the truth of HaShem and the denial of anything besides Him — as I explained in parashat Yitro regarding the verse ״אָנֹכִי ה׳ etc. אֲשֶׁר הוֹצֵאתִיךָ מֵאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם״ (Exodus 20:2). And since this is so, this dreamer should have nullified what the master of dreams would tell him in his false imaginings, because of what he saw while awake and not in a dream; and since he did not do so and came to recount it — this tells that the matter was justified in his eyes and that he wanted the house of Israel to heed him. Therefore he shall be put to death for his dreams and for his words." "<b>From the way that HaShem your Elohim has commanded you.</b> This comes to say that the reason for the death penalty for the prophet and the dreamer is because their words concern something that uproots the totality of the Torah — namely idolatry, which is weighed against the entire Torah. From this you learn that if he comes [with instructions to transgress] one of all the commandments of HaShem, whether a dreamer or a prophet, they are not executed. And the law of receiving [their instructions] from them: regarding the dreamer, he is told that he speaks falsely (שָׁוְא יְדַבֵּר), and the prophet — if he is known as a true prophet, we accept from him, like Elijah at Mount Carmel." "<b>And you shall burn the evil from your midst.</b> Perhaps it alludes to that which the Holy One, blessed be He, called by this name, as it is written (Genesis 8:21): ״יֵצֶר לֵב הָאָדָם רַע״ — and therefore it says <b>הָרָע</b>, the known [evil]. And the intention in this is that when they execute judgment in a case such as this, the evil is burned away and its power is uprooted and it will not grow strong in the hearts of the children of men, as it says, ״מִקִּרְבֶּךָ״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:17:14.095795131Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}